Problem:
        We  are  developing  UNIX  software   for   a   number   of    different
        architectures    (Sun,  Mips,  etc...)  and  operating  systems (Ultrix,
        SunOS 4.1.3, 5.3), simultaneously.   The  purpose  of this is two-fold:
        1.  This  assures  that  we   are   not   tied   into   one   particular
        hardware/software vendor, and
        2.  This  assures  that  our  software  actually   runs   on   all   the
        platforms it will be asked to run on.

        To achieve this, we are writing using only POSIX system calls, use   the
        GNU  compilers,  and  gnumake.  We have a system of home brewed sh, csh,
        and perl scripts which administrate  this.   All  our  files  are  under
        source code control (RCS control).

        When  our  binaries  are   made,   they   are   stored   in    different
        locations,     based   on   the   current   ARCH   and   OS,   and   the
        automounter

        While works, it is a bit of overhead for us  to   maintain   our  system
        as   new  OS's come along.  In addition, we have to add complexity (i.e.
        make rules) to it when we  have  to  add  new file types.

QUESTIONS:
