In <3apma3$lnd@ibch10.inf.tu-dresden.de> winkler@freia.inf.tu-dresden.de (Matthias Winkler) writes:
>a user who wants to work with DCE Security Services has to
>dce_login after logging into the system.  I've heard about
>a so called 'integrated login', that will you login into
>DCE Security as well as into the system.

At the first level, it's a fairly simple matter for a vendor to take their
login program and replace getpwnam()/crypt() calls with
sec_login_setup_identity/sec_login_validate_identity calls.  And make simlar
replacements to the su command, and perhaps change getpwnam() as well.
DCE source provides sample code and even tells how to use it (look at
one of the sec_login manpages I just mentioned).  The individual vendors
have to do this, of course, since OSF doesn't control /bin/login.
I don't know of any good technical reason why people haven't started doing
this as of DCE 1.0.3.

At the next level, you'd like to replace telnet, ftp, rsh, etc., with
equivalents that use DCE identities.  That's harder to do and requires
changing lots more source.
	/r$


