Another solution which works know is to use a yp->dce converter. Each
machine runs as a yp server and binds to itself. But it runs a special ypserv
which redirects your request trough DCE rpc (authentificated and so on) to
a main server. So the passwd and group maps can be gotten from the cds/rgy.
I've wrote most of the code and it works fine. It exports /etc/hosts, /
etc/services and so on as well. This is not as elagant as modifying libc.a
but it works now and is portable.

Instead of changing the login program (not a big job, anyway),  on AIX,
you can use authentification methods which allow you to specify which method 
is used and to give the program which implements it. It will be called by 
login.

>At the next level, you'd like to replace telnet, ftp, rsh, etc., with
>equivalents that use DCE identities.  That's harder to do and requires
>changing lots more source.
>	/r$

Yes, but how can you pass your ticket to another machine without the GSSAPI of 
DCE 1.1 ? And what about the RFC for "kerberized" versions of rlogin, telnet...

These thing are a big lack in DCE. For instance, with integrated login,
you can give "execute" permissions on command on a per-user basis on your
whole network. So the user log in, and can administrate some functions
with central authoriaztion, central logging and auditing without having to 
"su". (Im also writing the code, it works in alpha).

But without a special rlogin, telnet and so on, this rights will be lost  
when he goes to another machine. 

