>Another solution which works know is to use a yp->dce converter.

Neat hack -- can you make the code available?

>But without a special rlogin, telnet and so on, this rights will be lost  
>when he goes to another machine. 

Right, this is what I said in my original posting.  (More accurately, it's
not rights that are lost, but the DCE identity which the server maps into
a set of rights via an ACL.)

These utilities (only a DCE-based telnet would be needed; just require DFS :-)
can be written *now* without even requiring anything from DCE 1.1.  They
won't interoperate with standard telnet -- how could it? -- but it will
do other cool stuff (run over UDP with privacy).

It is not enough that OSF provides GSSAPI with DCE 1.1.  You need to specify
how to name "the telnet service on host foo.bar" (which the IETF draft does),
and then you might want to hack telnetd so that it doesn't prompt for a
password if you get a DCE PAC from the GSSAPI.

These are just some of the issues which make full single sign-on a complicated
issue.  One that OSF can't solve by itself.
	/r$
however, you want them to interoperate
I think that ideally you want to be able to say "host XXX"


