igoldber@vegas.jpmorgan.com (Ilya Goldberg)
writes:
|> I have set up CDS directories for users wishing
|> to put in their own
|> servers with their own CDS entries in a manner
|> similar to what's
|> described in John Dilley's paper "Practical
|> Experiences With the DCE
|> Cell Directory Service".  This involved
|> creating a /.:/home directory
|> and a subdirectory for each user.  I then
|> edited the ACL of each
|> user's directory to give them full access
|> (rwdtcia?).  However,
|> it turns out that although the users could
|> create their own server,
|> group, and profile entries, they could not
|> create subdirectories!
|> 
|> The reason users could not create CDS
|> subdirectories is that they need
|> write permission on the clearinghouse.  There
|> are many ways I can
|> grant the users this permission in the
|> clearninghouse ACL (add each
|> user separately, add the users' groups, or add
|> the write permission
|> to the any_other user entry).  The question is
|> -- should I?  Would
|> the users be able to do anything else that may
|> harm the system or
|> other users if they had this permission?
|> 
|> It seems that the requirement of having write
|> permission on the
|> clearinghouse to create subdirectories is
|> useless since the only
|> way to run things is to have it always turned
|> on.  Or does this
|> requirement try to help the administrator limit
|> which clearinghouses
|> can and which ones cannot be used to store
|> directory replicas by
|> groups of users?  Can a user create their own
|> clearinghouse for such
|> purposes?  Should the administrator create one
|> for them?  What are
|> you doing where you are?
|> 
|> -Ilya Goldberg
|> 
|> goldberg_ilya@jpmorgan.com

We use a group that has rwdt-i- (no ACL changes and no replica admin) rights
to the namespace, and rwdt- (no ACL changes) on the clearinghouse.

Don't forget to set up the initail container and inital object ACLs of the
user's home CDS directories.

The neat part about a user belonging to a group that has the requisite CDS
perms is that to change to perms, you only have to modifiy a single ACL.
Also, by removing a user from the group, you restrict that user from the CDS
in one fell swoop.  The down side is that it's difficult to customize access
for the users.  To achieve some differences, you could create a couple groups
-- each with a particular set of permissions -- and add each user to one of
the groups.  Watch out adding users to more than one group though: read about
how ACLs are searched and entries are matched so you aren't surprised.

Under very few circumstances would we want to grant individual users explicit
clearinghouse permissions.

-M


