In <39dlml$25a@lastactionhero.rs.itd.umich.edu> markc@citi.umich.edu writes:
>and we probably cannot change ACLs at the secondary site during the
>down time.

You can change ACLs on other servers, but you can't change ACLs on
security objects.

>Machines may not fair as well as users because machines change their
>passwords quite often

All that will happen is that you'll get an error message.  Nothing breaks
if the machine (sec_clientd) can't change its key.

>  Also, bear in mind that security
>replication can consume prodigious administrative time.

Why do you say that?  Assuming the hosts and network stay up, there's no
administrative overhead at all.

>OSF recommends that the information
>about a server that is kept in the namespace be persistent and not
>change very often.

Yah, well ... I've always felt that the namespace should accurately reflect
reality.  If a server shuts down cleanly it should remove itself from the
namespace.  The cost of doing this is incurred by the namespace.  If the
server doesn't remove itself, then the cost of false connection attempts
is incurred by all the clients.  However, the above recommendation is more
robust in the face of network lossage.

Hope this helps,
	/r$


