In <androidCyC7rD.MKv@netcom.com> android@netcom.com (Pramod Kalapa) writes:
> if((pthread_create(&thread_hdl1, pthread_attr_default,
>            (pthread_startroutine_t) open_file,
>            (pthread_addr_t)NULL)) != 0)

>However, the thread doesn't enter the function "open_file". I've stepped
>through the code with dbx and it just exits out of main.

Just to clarify, the thread that called pthread_create doesn't enter
open_file.  Instead a new "flow of execution" starts.  If your
main is doing
	pthread_create
	pthread_create
	exit
Then what is probably happening is that the three threads are running
just before exit, but then they're all shut down by the process exit.
You probably want to put
	pthread_join(&thread_hdl1);
	pthread_join(&thread_hdl2);
before your call to exit.  This will make sure that your process doesn't
go away until all the threads are finished.

As a side note, very few debuggers are aware of threads.  I don't know
about AIX's dbx (this is also an inside joke :-).  You might find it
easier to do something like
	 if((pthread_create(&thread_hdl1, pthread_attr_default,
	            open_file, "#1")) != 0)
	 if((pthread_create(&thread_hdl2, pthread_attr_default,
	            open_file, "#2")) != 0)
And write open_file as
	void *open_file(void *arg) {
	    if (debugging && arg != NULL) {
		fprintf(stderr, "open_file %s started\n", (char *)arg);
		fflush(stderr);
	    }
	    ... real body of the thread ...
	}


