In article <39lsjk$lrf@clam.hi.com> wright@clam.hi.com (David Wright) writes:
>The single biggest problem is errno handling.  Errno is a single,
>global variable in standard Unix.  (Granted, this was a really bad
>design decision, but we're stuck with it.)  Since DCE applications are
>multi-threaded, they need to be able to keep errno on a per-thread
>basis (so they don't trip over each other's values).

If you read the definition of errno in the ANSI standard, you'll see that
errno can be a #define that evaluates to an lvalue.  This means you
can do things like this:
    /* Returns the address of the per-thread "errno" field in the current
     * thread.  Assumes each thread has an internal state struct. */
    extern int *pthread_errno_np(void);

    #define errno  (*pthread_errno_np())

In practice, however, it's probably easier to just store errno in the internal
per-thread state block and copy it in/out as part of your thread task
context switch.

For kernel threads, the kernel should just be doing the right thing.
	/r$


