Paul Leach, an Architect at Microsoft, gave a presentation at the DCE SIG
the other week.  He's given me permission to summarize his slides.  His
talk was on Microsoft and DCE.

MS RPC is interoperable with DCE RPC.  The next version will support almost
everything missing from the current version:  connectionless, authenticated
and encrypted RPC, management API, full pointers, rpc_sm_allocate, and
cancels.  There are a few missing parts: pipes, and connectionless doesn't
do authentication and connectionless context handles don't interoperate.
These will be fixed.  Pickling (a DCE 1.1 feature) and a DCE API portability
header file are also supported.  RPC runtime will be bundled in W/NT and
Windows 95 -- quoting Paul, "millions & millions of DCE RPC clients."

MS RPC is used for NT interoperability: registry, printing, Exchange (MS
Mail), netware-based authentcation, etc.  Microsoft will have Macintosh
client for DCE RPC, available in mid-95.   Again, Paul's slide: "No major
platforms without DCE RPC available!"

COM is Microsoft's object model; OLE is an instantiation of that model.
There are 400+ OLE 2.0 applications shipping (millions of copies).
Microsoft is using OLE/COM in all areas: operating systems, consumer,
multimedia, databases, tools, etc.  It's an open architecture: all interfaces
and protocols will be published.  Objects are defined and distributed
using extended DCE-style IDL, and interoperable RPC.  OLE 2.x, distributed
COM, will be available on UNIX, OS/2, and legacy platforms from Microsoft's
partners and can be implemented on top of an unmodified DCE RPC runtime.

Please don't ask me any more about this -- I just posted all I know.
	/r$


