: >For kernel threads, the kernel should just be doing the right thing.

: How can a the kernel do this?  If errno is a statica variable,
: the kernel cannot move it about in a transparent way.
: Multpiple threads on multiple processors will want to read
: the variable so without #define errno (*(___errno())) I don't
: see a way of doing it.

In fact, at least one kernel level threads implementation (Solaris') does
use the '#define errno' trick.  Mainly, in /usr/include/errno.h, it has
the following:

extern int *___errno();
#define errno (*(___errno()))


So every time the process looks at errno, it's really looking at *(___errno()),
which, if you look at carefully, evaluates to a lhs.  Presumably, 
___errno() returns a pointer to the thread-specific errno and there's a errno
maintained by the kernel for each thread.

It seems like in this implementation, there's no notion of errno as a
process-wide global.

