These are good questions, and I concur that the documentation is sparse
about what actually happens in this situation.  Essentially, you ask
these two questions:

  1) What happens to the security service in the portion of the cell
  that is equipped with a secondary security server if that portion is
  separated from the rest of the cell by a network outage?

  2) What happens to the namespace service in the portion of the cell
  that is equipped with a additional CDS server if that portion is
  separated from the rest of the cell by a network outage?

I think it is important to separate the security and CDS because these
services behave differently, and the concepts and administrative tasks
of replication are also different.

I hope the following information is helpful.

PHYSICAL LOCATIONS:

In your message, when you say "where a cell spans multiple physical
locations" I do not know whether this means several floors of a
building, or a WAN.  OSF recommends that cells be LAN-based.  Putting a
single cell together on a large WAN is not a good idea practically for
several reasons, and I would recommend you create several smaller cells
instead.  I think intercell communication is more practical than
replication for linking physically disparate locations together, but
some DCE architectures can conflict with corporate culture and you may
have to replicate.

It is better to use intercell communication than to use replication for
gluing physically disparate locations together.  Replication is still a
good idea, even in the LAN-based cell.

REGISTRY REPLICATION:

We know that secondary security servers always maintain a slave
(read-only copy) of the registry and ACL databases for the cell.
Updates of the master DBs are sent from the primary security server to
the secondary servers via a propagation queuing mechanism.  We know
that one can designate that a particular secondary security server's
slave database be made the master, but there is always only one master
database at a time.  Therefore, if links between a primary and
secondary security server are broken, no writes can be sent to the
primary server -- and no changes made at the primary site will be
propagated to the secondary site -- until the connection is restored.
Users at the secondary site can still dce_login (using a pre-existing
account of course) to maintain authentication and ACLs can be evaluated
(read) to enable authorization.  We certainly cannot add any new users,
and we probably cannot change ACLs at the secondary site during the
down time.

Machines may not fair as well as users because machines change their
passwords quite often (every 10 minutes or so, as I recall), and this
password change obviously requires a write.  Local keytab files
(/krb5/v5srvtab) are available to the hosts at the secondary site, but
I do not think a writeable key service will be available.  I often
wonder what will happen to services running at the secondary site.

We have not worked with replicated security servers here yet, but I am
interested in what happens in this situation.  I would like to know for
sure that people (and machines) at the secondary site can continue to
work.  I would like to see how long it takes changes at the primary
site to reach the secondary site after the connection is restored.

In summary, replicating the security service is supposed to allow the
cut off cell to work normally, but I am not sure how periodic machine
password changes are handled.  Also, bear in mind that security
replication can consume prodigious administrative time.

CDS:

Namespace replication differs from security service replication.
Additional CDS servers do not necessarily maintain "read-only"
information because CDS replication actually takes place at the level
of the CDS directory.

Creating a CDS directory results in the creation of a "master replica"
in the server's clearinghouse.  The use of the term master "replica"
can be very misleading because master replicas are always created when
a CDS directory is created, even when there is only one CDS server, and
CDS replication is not yet established.  (I wish another term could
have been used, and I am always glad I did not have to write the DCE
documentation!)

After CDS replication is established (i.e., more than one CDS server is
running, and each is maintaining its own clearinghouse), read-only
replicas are created from the master replicas.  These read-only
replicas are stored in a different clearinghouse from where the master
replica is stored.  CDS Replication is a result of storing a master and
a read-only replica (CDS directory) in separate places (clearinghouses)
on separate hosts.

The twist is that the master replica can be created in any
clearinghouse in the cell.  When one creates a directory, the master
replica is automatically placed in a default (which is usually the
first) clearinghouse.  One can override this behavior and specify where
to place the master replica when one create the CDS directory:

 % cdscp create dir /.:/foo clearinghouse /.:/second_ch

This means that the system administrator can locate "local" portions of
the namespace on the closest clearinghouse and, in theory, eliminate or
at least mitigate some of the namespace problems created when the cell
network is divided.

Finally, to server registration.  OSF recommends that the information
about a server that is kept in the namespace be persistent and not
change very often.  (We do not rename things in real life very often
either.)  When one bring up a server on host A, the server can
automatically export it's bindings to a CDS entry indicating to other's
that the service is available on host A.  Alternatively, a system
administrator can add the server entry to the namespace as part of
service configuration.

Whether the service is running or down, the server entry can remain in
the namespace because the server entry only contains the protocol
sequence and host address.  No port numbers are included.  When the
service is runing on Host A, Host A's endpoint map will contain valid
endpoints, and a client can construct a complete binding using
namespace APIs to find out what host is running the service and the
port number on that host.  This is analogous to [always] knowing where
someone lives, and how to get there (ip address and protocol sequence),
but having to check whether they are home at any given time with a
telephone call.

In summary, if one plan where to put master replicas (storing "local"
namespace entries on the "local" clearinghouse), and export only
incomplete binding information to the namespace, one can see that a
secondary CDS server can provide acceptable name resolution while cut
off from the cell's primary CDS server.  The primary and secondary
servers will not be able to talk to each other, and no skulks can
occur, but users and services at the secondary site can still use (and
change) "local" namespace resources.

We have limited experience with namespace replication here, but two
things I know for sure:  One must plan namespace configuration
carefully, and one should count on spending considerable sys admin time
administrating CDS replication.

-Mark


