Mr Mike James Lijewski (lijewski@HK.Super.NET) wrote:

: Appended is a shar file of a simple DCE RPC application.  It exhibits
: what I consider very poor performance when both client and server are
: run on the same machine.  If I run one server and three clients on a
: four processor DEC Alpha 2100/500 I only see about 800 RPCs/second.
: This is no higher than if I run the server on the 2100/500 and a
: client on each of three separate workstations.  It would appear that
: the RPC runtime together with the networking code in the kernel is
: the bottleneck.  Anyone care to comment?

I've worked a lot with DCE and in particular with performance.
First, note that DCE RPC uses the exact same implementation for local and
remote RPCs.  This implementation assumes a lot of process context swaps
during the RPC protocol.  We found that because of the context swap overhead,
the protocol ran no faster locally than remotely.  This is exactly what you
saw.

I'm not familiar with a 2100/500.  If you are running OSF/1 and if DCE is using
true kernel threads, then I'm a bit surprised that the performance is
unimproved.  If its Ultrix or Co-Routines, then it fits the my performance
model.

Enjoy,
Eliot jaffe




