I have set up CDS directories for users wishing to put in their own
servers with their own CDS entries in a manner similar to what's
described in John Dilley's paper "Practical Experiences With the DCE
Cell Directory Service".  This involved creating a /.:/home directory
and a subdirectory for each user.  I then edited the ACL of each
user's directory to give them full access (rwdtcia?).  However,
it turns out that although the users could create their own server,
group, and profile entries, they could not create subdirectories!

The reason users could not create CDS subdirectories is that they need
write permission on the clearinghouse.  There are many ways I can
grant the users this permission in the clearninghouse ACL (add each
user separately, add the users' groups, or add the write permission
to the any_other user entry).  The question is -- should I?  Would
the users be able to do anything else that may harm the system or
other users if they had this permission?

It seems that the requirement of having write permission on the
clearinghouse to create subdirectories is useless since the only
way to run things is to have it always turned on.  Or does this
requirement try to help the administrator limit which clearinghouses
can and which ones cannot be used to store directory replicas by
groups of users?  Can a user create their own clearinghouse for such
purposes?  Should the administrator create one for them?  What are
you doing where you are?

